      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Niles Canyon Railway’s Sunol Depot, built in 1884, is the last surviving example of Southern Pacific’s standard design.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.ktsfgo.com/en/post/20240718-discover-sunol-en",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Sunol was named after Don Antonio Suñol, the 19th‑century ranch owner of Rancho Valle de San José.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunol%2C_California",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1884 Thomas Foxwell Bachelder sold land that became the core of Sunol, sparking the town’s early growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunol%2C_California",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Union Pacific and Southern Pacific once ran parallel tracks through Sunol until 1984, when the Southern Pacific line was abandoned.",
